      - OCaml target parsing now supports effect-handler cases, retaining effect arguments, continuation bindings, guards, and handler bodies. Direct effect-handler syntax in rule patterns remains unsupported. Before: The parser at upstream revision 4a885a615634dd447ae6b9d8f2cee4a3ea495087 produced ERROR nodes for a minimal effect-handler case, and the unchanged Menhir fallback rejected it. The updated target parser produces a clean AST with both effect and continuation patterns. Evidence: `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/effect_handlers.ml`, `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/effect_handlers.ast.expect`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/effect_handler_body.ml`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/effect_handler_body.sgrep`
      - OCaml target parsing now supports labeled tuple expressions, binding and match patterns, and types, including shorthand and typed components. Direct labeled-tuple syntax in rule patterns remains unsupported. Before: At upstream revision 4a885a615634dd447ae6b9d8f2cee4a3ea495087, a minimal labeled tuple produced parser ERROR nodes and was rejected by the unchanged Menhir fallback. The updated conversion retains labels, component values, types, and binding patterns. Evidence: `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/labeled_tuples.ml`, `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/labeled_tuples.ast.expect`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/labeled_tuple_children.ml`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/labeled_tuple_children.sgrep`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/labeled_tuple_distinct.ml`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/labeled_tuple_distinct.sgrep`
      - OCaml target parsing now accepts local opens in types, including dotted module paths and applicative functor paths. Existing limitations in polymorphic-variant and package-type AST detail remain. Before: A local type open such as type t = M.(u * v) produced ERROR nodes with upstream revision 4a885a615634dd447ae6b9d8f2cee4a3ea495087 and was rejected by the unchanged Menhir fallback. The updated converter retains the opened path, including functor arguments, and the inner type. Evidence: `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/local_open_types.ml`, `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/local_open_types.ast.expect`
      - OCaml target parsing now accepts short dependent functor types such as (X : S) -> S. Detailed module-type matching and this syntax in rule patterns remain unsupported. Before: The short dependent functor-type probe produced ERROR nodes with upstream revision 4a885a615634dd447ae6b9d8f2cee4a3ea495087 and was rejected by the unchanged Menhir fallback. The updated target parser accepts it; module-type AST detail remains at its existing limited level. Evidence: `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/short_functor_types.mli`, `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/short_functor_types.ast.expect`
      - OCaml target parsing now accepts quoted strings whose Unicode delimiters use equivalent composed and decomposed spellings, preserving their literal contents. Before: A quoted string using composed à in its opening delimiter and decomposed à in its closing delimiter produced ERROR nodes with upstream revision 4a885a615634dd447ae6b9d8f2cee4a3ea495087; the unchanged Menhir fallback also rejected it. The updated parser produces the expected string literal. Evidence: `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/quoted_literals_extensions.ml`, `OSS/tests/parsing/ocaml/quoted_literals_extensions.ast.expect`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/unicode_quoted_string.ml`, `OSS/tests/patterns/ocaml/unicode_quoted_string.sgrep`
